# In-product firmware update (CRA Annex I #7): SWUpdate, console-driven.
# Signature verification (CMS via OpenSSL) is configured in the package
# build config below; the embedded webserver stays OFF — the product web
# consoles drive updates through their own authenticated CGIs, and the
# firewall ships default-deny (media/joral/swupdate-implementation-plan.md).
# A/B on SPI NAND: SWUpdate's ubivol handler needs libmtd/libubi, which the
# mtd package installs to staging (MTD_INSTALL_STAGING=YES). Without this the
# swupdate build silently omits the handler -- its own Config.in says "Select
# BR2_PACKAGE_MTD if you want to use swupdate with UBI partitions" -- and a
# .swu aimed at a UBI volume fails at install time, not build time.
# Only the four tools the boot path actually calls. Annex I #4 is about attack
# surface, and these manipulate raw flash: ubiformat and ubirmvol can destroy a
# running system. Enabling the whole mtd suite (13 binaries) as a side effect of
# an update feature is exactly the kind of unexamined growth the gate exists to
# catch, so the set is pinned to what S20linkmount uses:
#   ubiattach  -- attach the MTD partition, every boot
#   ubiformat  -- recovery fallback when attach fails (S20linkmount:83)
#   ubimkvol   -- create the volume on a first boot / after format
#   ubiblock   -- the read-only block node the rootfs is mounted through
#   ubinfo     -- READ-ONLY: reports device/volume geometry and data_bytes.
#                 Added 2026-09-04 for A/B field diagnosis on the Max: after a
#                 .swu install the question "did the standby volume actually
#                 get written" has no other answer on the unit, and the sysfs
#                 substitute (/sys/class/ubi/ubiX_Y/{name,data_bytes}) has to
#                 be assembled by hand across two directory levels. It opens
#                 the UBI device O_RDONLY and issues no write ioctl, so it is
#                 not a raw-flash writer and does not carry the risk the rest
#                 of this trim exists to exclude.
# SWUpdate itself shells out to NONE of these; it links libubi directly, which
# is why the ubivol handler needs the LIBRARY but not the binaries.
#
# ubiupdatevol is deliberately NOT added, though it is the other tool a bench
# operator reaches for. It writes a volume and can truncate one (-t), which is
# exactly the raw-flash-writer class named below; the ubivol handler performs
# the real update through libubi without it, so on a shipped image it would be
# attack surface serving no shipped function. Bench rollback testing corrupts a
# standby slot with ubirmvol, which is already here.
BR2_PACKAGE_MTD=y
BR2_PACKAGE_MTD_UBIATTACH=y
BR2_PACKAGE_MTD_UBIBLOCK=y
BR2_PACKAGE_MTD_UBIFORMAT=y
BR2_PACKAGE_MTD_UBIMKVOL=y
BR2_PACKAGE_MTD_UBINFO=y
# Everything else in the mtd suite is DISABLED EXPLICITLY. buildroot defaults
# these to y, so omitting a symbol does NOT turn it off -- the first attempt at
# this trim did exactly that and shipped all 13 UBI tools plus nandwrite,
# nanddump, flash_erase and mtd_debug. Raw-flash writers on a shipped image are
# an Annex I #4 attack-surface regression, so each one is named:
# BR2_PACKAGE_MTD_DOCFDISK is not set
